A render slot re-renders on what its structure reads, and a prop inside its
body is patched in place, like a prop outside one.

The slot for `if shown` used to re-fire whenever anything its body read
changed, and a re-render builds the body afresh -- so every tick of `uptime`
destroyed the panel and built a new one, and with it anything the widgets held
that no state described: focus, a selection, the text typed into an unbound
input. The slot now re-renders when `shown` changes and when `items` does.

What the body reads beyond that is the body's own: each top-level node there
that reads state is lifted into a component built at run time
(passSlotBodyInstances), so its widgets live in an instance the slot keeps, and
a write to `uptime` hands the live instances their new value through the
setter -- the same route a prop of a runtime instance outside a slot takes.
The loop's rows are the same shape, one instance per row, each patched where
it stands.

bubbletea and android re-render the view from state on every change, so they
have nothing to patch and are here to show their output is unchanged.

deny out/go/fyne/model.go `uptime__inst0 \+= 1\n\s*m\.__renderSlot` -- a tick patches the live instances and re-renders no slot
deny out/go/gtk4/model.go `uptime__inst0 \+= 1\n\s*m\.__renderSlot` -- as above, on gtk4
deny out/none/html/index.html `uptime__inst0 \+= 1;\n\s*__renderSlot` -- as above, on html

-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        fyne
        gtk4
        bubbletea
    }
    none { html }
    kotlin { android }
}

component main node {
    var shown = true
    var uptime = 0
    var items = ["a", "b"]

    vbox {
        button #tick(text="tick", @click { uptime += 1 })
        button #flip(text="flip", @click { shown = !shown })
        if shown {
            vbox {
                text(value="static")
                text(value="up {uptime}")
            }
        }
        for var it = items {
            text(value="{it} at {uptime}")
        }
    }
}

window {
    main
}
